    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d90">

                <pb facs="0206" n="94" xml:id="pg94"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 836/53–54.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to Chargé Lorillard</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1907-02-21"><hi rendition="#italic">February 21,
                        1907</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 49.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    dispatch No. 96, of the 14th ultimo, embodying telegraphic correspondence in
                    connection the complaint of Henry Forster &amp; Co., of Pernambuco, and
                    transmitting a copy of a decree of January 10, 1907, continuing, from January 1,
                    1907, the 20 per cent preferential reduction on certain American goods.</p>
                <p>Your action, taken on the complaint of Messrs. Forster &amp; Co., is approved by
                    the department, and you will ask the return to the importers of any excess in
                    duties above the preferential rates that may be brought to your attention as
                    having been collected in January on shipments of American products entitled to
                    preferential treatment.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Bacon.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
